WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:11.350
ANDI GUTMANS: Hi, everyone.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.140
I'm Andi Gutmans,
general manager

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.040
and VP of engineering for
databases at Google Cloud.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.680
Thank you for joining us
for Google Cloud Next.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.560
We have some exciting product
announcements to share with you

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.280
in this session.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.200
Digital innovators
are disrupting

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.530
traditional industries in new
ways because of their ability

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:34.670
to use data and software to
deliver new digital customer

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.330
experiences.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.450
Customers expect personalized
and seamless experiences

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.100
across their devices, in all
places, and at all times.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.250
To deliver these next-generation
digital experiences,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.190
developers need to be
empowered to build apps faster

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.710
by leveraging fully
managed databases,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.080
use their existing
skills and investments,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.930
and build their apps for
success with databases that

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.990
were designed to power the
most demanding workloads

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.150
in the world.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.710
We offer best-in-class databases
for always-on applications,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.430
with global reach
and unlimited scale.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.780
Google Cloud is committed to
open source and open standards.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.490
We offer managed
services that are

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.970
fully compatible with the most
popular open-source engines,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:23.450
such as MySQL,
Postgres, and Redis.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.100
We manage the complexity
of running your database

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.940
so that they are simple to
use and operate, and make

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.790
app development faster
and easier for you.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.150
Leading organizations in
all regions and industries

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.640
have placed their
trust in our databases.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.760
And our databases remain
one of the fastest growing

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.860
segments of Google Cloud.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.500
In the newly
published Gartner 2021

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.680
"Scorecard" for operational
databases, Google Cloud

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.020
received an overall
score of 90 out of 100,

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.070
which is higher than the score
of the largest alternative

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.540
cloud provider.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.250
Google Cloud met 100% of the
required criteria outlined

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.390
by Gartner.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.590
Also, Gartner predicts
that 75% of databases

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.520
will be deployed or migrated to
a cloud platform by next year.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.940
Imagine 40 years
of databases placed

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.780
on-premises being superseded
by cloud databases

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.710
in such a short duration.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.100
It's amazing.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.900
Our customers'
journey to undergo

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.600
data-driven transformation
typically involves three steps.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.710
The first step,
migrate, is there

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.140
to help customers move
existing applications

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.520
and databases to the cloud,
frequently on a tight timeline.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.440
Customers need a
fast-track approach

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.590
to lift and shift what they're
running today to the cloud.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.550
This as-is migration already
adds tremendous value,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.860
even if it doesn't
provide the full benefits

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.610
of cloud-native capabilities.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.950
Once migrated, many
of our customers

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.650
seek to modernize their
database environments

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.920
by transitioning off
expensive legacy databases

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.130
and onto open-source databases.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.460
For customers looking to build
next-gen applications entirely

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.520
in the cloud, they're
in the transform phase.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.580
This is about unlocking
new possibilities

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.530
in competitive differentiation.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.080
It's about delivering new
transformational customer

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.010
experiences and innovative
business models.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.340
For your existing databases
that you need to easily migrate

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.230
to a fully-managed
cloud service,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:34.490
we offer Cloud SQL, with
full compatibility for MySQL,

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.800
Postgres, and SQL Server.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.800
It is one of the fastest growing
large services in Google Cloud.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.960
Cloud SQL offers
easy integration

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.750
with existing apps and
Google Cloud Services,

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:50.770
like Google Kubernetes
Engine and BigQuery.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:57.220
More than 650,000 GKE pods
securely connect to Cloud SQL.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:01.540
And BigQuery users
query over 125 petabytes

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.480
of data in Cloud SQL
on average in a month,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.590
using our federated
querying capabilities.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.170
We've invested heavily
in improving Cloud SQL

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.040
and making it enterprise-ready
for your demanding workloads.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.279
In the past 12 months, we
have introduced capabilities,

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:21.880
such as cross-region replicas,
point-in-time recovery,

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.720
customer-managed encryption
keys, VPC Service Controls,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.370
and IAM support.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.040
We have significantly improved
the maintenance experience

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.980
for customers and given
them maintenance controls.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.320
Cloud SQL maintenance
is, on average,

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.800
80% shorter than it
was 12 months ago.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.230
And maintenance for
My SQL and Postgres

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.230
is now shorter than that
of competing offerings

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.330
from other cloud
providers, based

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.330
on figures published in
online documentation as

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.400
of August 2021.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.030
Earlier this year, we launched
industry-leading observability

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.640
with Cloud SQL
Insights for Postgres.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.760
It empowers developers to
address database performance

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.120
issues themselves at
no additional cost.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.670
This has become one
of the fastest adopted

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.670
new capabilities in Cloud
SQL, and we look forward

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.070
to delivering
equivalent functionality

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.730
across more database engines.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.600
In addition, we recently
introduced Cloud SQL Cost

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.350
Recommenders with Active
Assist that empowers developers

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.380
to better manage costs.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.620
We have made migrations to
Cloud SQL easier and faster

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.980
with the Database
Migration Service.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.430
This offers a simple experience
with minimal downtime

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.660
for reliable and predictable
migrations to Cloud SQL.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.080
It is serverless,
secure, and offered

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.160
at no additional charge.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.950
More than 85% of all migrations
are underway in under an hour,

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.860
and a majority of customers
are migrating databases

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.290
from other clouds.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.180
Database Migration
Service currently

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.910
supports MySQL and
Postgres databases,

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:09.260
with support for SQL Server
migrations coming soon.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.170
For customers bringing Oracle
workloads to Google Cloud,

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.540
we offer our bare metal
solution for Oracle

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.720
that allows you to run Oracle
databases the same way you

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.510
do it on-premises,
but cost-effectively

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:23.120
and with less than 2
milliseconds latency

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.060
to all of your GCP services.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.460
Bare metal solution
enables you to continue

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.390
using your existing Oracle
technologies, such as RAC,

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.510
Data Guard, and RMAN.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.390
For workloads that require
microsecond latency,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.130
we offer Memorystore.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.790
Memorystore is fully compatible
with Redis and Memcached,

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.850
and offers an easy migration
destination for your Redis

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.860
and Memcached workloads.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.090
Memorystore is a scalable,
secure, highly available

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.830
in-memory service that is used
by 90% of the top 100 Cloud

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.590
customers.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.440
We recently announced a
preview of Datastream,

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:06.370
a serverless change data
capture and replication service.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.700
With Datastream, you can
easily and seamlessly

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:14.080
deliver change data streams from
your Oracle and MySQL databases

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:19.300
into Google Cloud Services, such
as BigQuery, Cloud SQL, Google

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.490
Cloud Storage, and Spanner.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.590
The serverless
architecture of Datastream

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:27.860
transparently scales up
or down, as data volumes

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.360
shift in real time.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.880
Now let's discuss our
transformative databases

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.730
and how they can help you
transform your business

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:39.780
models and the digital
experiences for your users.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.090
Today, your business-critical
applications

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.220
may need to serve millions
of users across the globe

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:48.180
and process data volumes that
are an order of magnitude

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.790
higher than just
a few years ago.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.660
New, always-on business models
require these applications

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.280
to be reliable, secure,
and continuously available.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.460
Your applications need to
scale seamlessly and sustain

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.450
high performance, even during
unexpected spikes in demand.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.870
We give you access to the same
databases that power the most

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:15.700
demanding Google applications,
like Search, YouTube, Gmail,

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.980
Maps, and Payments.

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:19.900
Google had to invent
these databases

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.150
because traditional
databases were not

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.210
designed to handle
these demands for scale,

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.140
availability, and performance.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:32.250
We offer not one, but three
such transformative databases--

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:36.179
Spanner, Bigtable,
and Firestore.

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.309
All three of them
have a common set

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:41.070
of highly differentiated
core attributes,

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.740
like industry-leading
five 9s availability

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.930
SLAs, no maintenance
downtime, unlimited scale,

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:53.310
automatic sharding,
zero-touch global replication,

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:57.030
automatic failover,
superior price-performance,

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:01.110
and enhanced security
and compliance.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.630
Spanner is our fully
managed relational database

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.550
that offers the
familiarity and developer

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.560
experience of a
relational database

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.420
and the scalability
of a NoSQL database.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.630
Spanner enables unlimited
scale, so you can easily

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:18.320
accommodate future growth
without painful architecting,

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.180
adjust your capacity on-the-fly,
and pay for only what you use.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:26.150
It allows you to deliver
high-performance transactions

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:30.440
with strong consistency
across regions and continents,

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.950
so you can simplify
operations and place data

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.350
close to your users.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:39.830
Our enterprise customers in
financial services, gaming,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:43.460
retail, technology, and
many other industries

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.130
run some of their most
demanding operational workloads

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.870
on Spanner.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:49.580
With Spanner, you
can spend less time

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.620
worrying about how to
scale your database

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:55.070
and instead focus on
scaling your business.

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.230
As wonderful and
unique as Spanner is,

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:01.330
it wasn't the most accessible
database for developers.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.210
We wanted developers to be able
to start small with Spanner

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.610
so they could design their
applications for success

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:08.630
from the get-go.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.820
To this end, we
recently introduced

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.250
more granular instance
sizing for Spanner

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.770
to democratize access to it.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:18.200
With more granular
instance sizing,

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.620
you can start at 1/10 the
cost of regular instances,

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:26.380
equating to approximately
$65 per month.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.860
This means you can
start small and easily

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.950
scale your Spanner instance
as your workload grows,

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.360
with no downtime.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.120
Developers love the scale
and availability of Spanner,

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.190
but they wanted to take
advantage of Spanner

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:44.890
using their familiar open tools
and their existing skill set.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.550
Therefore, to continue this
theme of democratizing access

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.740
to Spanner, we're
excited to announce

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.500
the preview of a Postgres
interface for Spanner,

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.230
starting today.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:58.600
This empowers developers
to take advantage

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.720
of Google's transformative
relational database

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.170
using standard tools and skills
from the popular Postgres

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.140
ecosystem.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.020
This interface combines
the power and reliability

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:14.490
of Spanner with the familiarity
and portability of Postgres

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:17.460
to enhance flexibility.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.530
By providing a
Postgres interface,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.620
we're democratizing access
to Spanner for millions

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.260
of developers and ISVs.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.860
Starting today, you can
create Spanner databases that

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.350
use PostgreSQL
dialect and connect

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.120
with its OpenWire protocol.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.120
This interface supports
Spanner's rich feature

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:41.510
set using most popular Postgres
data types and SQL features.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.310
Postgres interface
for Spanner is

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:47.670
available at no
additional charge.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.250
What this means is that for
your Postgres workloads,

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:55.170
we offer you not one, but
two fully managed services.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:58.380
For maximum compatibility
with open-source Postgres

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.050
and for the easiest
lift-and-shift migrations,

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.990
you have Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.640
For your most
demanding workloads

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:09.600
where you need unlimited scale
and five 9s availability SLAs,

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.450
we offer Spanner with its
new Postgres interface

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.150
that allows you to leverage
your skills and investments

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.930
in the Postgres ecosystem.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:23.400
We're super excited about
our rich Postgres offerings.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.070
Now for apps that require
consistent single-digit

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.520
millisecond latencies and need
to support millions of requests

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.400
per second, we offer Bigtable.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:35.280
Bigtable is our fully
managed NoSQL database

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.950
for large operational
and analytical workloads.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.770
And it's HBase-compatible.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.860
Bigtable is uniquely capable
at powering use cases

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.890
like personalization at
scale and fraud detection.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.800
We're excited to share that
autoscaling is coming soon

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.550
to Bigtable.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.520
This will allow Bigtable to
automatically add or remove

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:59.210
capacity in response to changing
demands of your workloads,

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:03.030
so you'll only pay for
the capacity you need.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.360
We talked about the
importance of the velocity

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.610
of your application iteration.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.560
For rapid app
development, we offer

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.500
Firestore, our fully
managed scalable

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:15.050
serverless document database.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:17.450
Firestore is beloved
by developers because

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.690
of its ability to serve
both as a document database

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:23.320
and a backend as a service.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:25.330
Firestore enables
offline support

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.810
with real-time synchronization.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:30.490
An example of how
much developers

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:32.350
love Firestore is
that Firestore has

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:38.600
more than 250,000 monthly
active developers and Firestore

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:43.340
apps power more than 750
million monthly active users

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.490
using Firebase authentication.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:49.700
Not only is Firestore
extremely developer-friendly,

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.370
but we're also focusing
on making Firestore

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:55.160
more enterprise-friendly.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.140
We're introducing a
number of enhancements

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.730
to Firestore for greater
enterprise readiness.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:05.060
With that, I'd like to introduce
one of our most innovative

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.910
customers who has
successfully accomplished

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:10.370
a data-driven cloud
transformation.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:15.620
It is my pleasure to invite
Kumar Menon, SVP of data fabric

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:19.770
and decision science
technology at Equifax.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.360
Kumar, thank you for
joining us today.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.590
We would love to hear
about Equifax's innovation

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:29.950
that you and your team have been
driving in partnership with us.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.260
KUMAR MENON: Thank you, Andi.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:35.250
I wanted to start off by giving
you some background on Equifax

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.520
and the transformation
we are under.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.950
Equifax is a leading data
analytics and technology

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:44.230
company and is one of the three
biggest credit rating agencies.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.220
We are present in 24
countries worldwide.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.920
In 2018, we embarked on
a massive company-wide

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.800
transformation to
modernize our technology.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:56.040
This was a result of an
unfortunate breach incident

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.770
in 2017, which brought
the global spotlight

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.690
onto our company.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:03.210
Due to the oversight that
was placed on us and the need

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:06.120
for us to ensure we are good
custodians of the high-fidelity

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.345
data we have, we realized
that patching and modifying

00:15:09.345 --> 00:15:13.940
our legacy systems would not
be a sustainable solution.

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:16.460
We made the bold decision to
adopt a cloud-first strategy

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.770
and rewrite all of
our data platforms

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:20.790
and associated
applications, leveraging

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:23.390
cloud-native architecture.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:24.980
Our legacy systems
were operating

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.350
in silos, which made it
difficult to effectively

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.360
innovate and monetize
our differentiated data

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:33.697
assets through new
multi-data products.

00:15:33.697 --> 00:15:35.280
So we also took this
as an opportunity

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:39.360
to reorganize our data under a
common enterprise domain model,

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.670
onto a single
global data fabric,

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.800
with the security and
automated governance baked

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.830
into the architecture.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.440
The goal was to make it easier
for our data and analytics

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:50.750
and product teams to
innovate and deliver

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:52.970
new products
faster, thus helping

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:55.190
our customers and consumers
make better decisions.

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.850
Here's what our end-to-end
data flow looks like.

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:04.953
The data fabric is at the heart
of the Equifax value chain.

00:16:04.953 --> 00:16:07.120
If you look at this
illustration from left to right,

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.420
the data fabric helps us ingest
different domains of data

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:14.090
we bring into our ecosystem
in a consistent, reliable,

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:18.380
highly-performant, governed,
compliant, and secure manner,

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:21.160
so that our product, data and
analytics, and business teams

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.950
can leverage the synergies
of all the data assets

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:27.780
to innovate and deliver products
that our customers need.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:31.470
In short, we want global
data at our fingertips.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:35.050
Equifax Cloud will enable our
product innovation, growth,

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:37.140
and competitiveness.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.630
The data fabric platform is
pivotal to the Equifax business

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:42.020
model going forward.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:45.080
Why is the Equifax data
fabric a gamechanger?

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:47.240
Let me give you a few examples.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:49.940
Equifax has moved from
80-plus data silos,

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.680
enabling multiple
sources of data

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:55.550
to be standardized and organized
into a seamless data fabric

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.550
with logical separation
in governing groups.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.460
The structure drastically
simplifies our architecture

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:05.630
and gives Equifax the
ability to act quickly

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:10.349
to address customer,
regulatory, and security needs.

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:13.130
We will achieve bolt-on
mergers and acquisitions

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.530
with better and faster
integrations than ever before,

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:21.430
accelerating our
growth tremendously.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.760
Now let's look at a more
technical view of the data

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.240
fabric and see how we have
leveraged GCP-managed database

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:30.390
services to deliver our
analytics and operational

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:31.500
workloads.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.770
Let me break those down
for you from top to bottom.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.300
The data fabric
services shown here

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.640
help curate the
raw data we collect

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:44.050
across the various domains, such
as credit, employment, wealth,

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:47.060
utilities, et cetera,
to name a few.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:48.880
We then key and
link the data, which

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.980
is a form of entity
resolution process

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:55.370
that helps us identify a single
unique instance of an entity--

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.240
for example, a unique
consumer identity--

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:01.150
across all the domains
of data we collect.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:03.790
This data is then made
available for predictive models

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:07.840
and real-time decisioning as a
set of purpose-specific views

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:09.610
with the regulatory
rules applied,

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:11.290
thus ensuring permissible use.

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.730
This low-latency,
high-value ecosystem

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:20.450
leverages Cloud BigTable
as the core Datastore

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:23.570
and the pipelines are
built in Cloud Dataflow.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:26.030
The Cloud BigTable
scalable architecture

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:28.670
and its support for
customer-managed encryption

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:31.850
keys help us provide a
resilient, reliable, and secure

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:35.420
data platform to support highly
stringent millisecond SLAs

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:37.930
that our customers
demand from us.

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:40.480
The data fabric
is tightly coupled

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.990
with our Ignite and our
analytics services, which

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:46.750
is used by our data scientists
for feature engineering

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:48.700
and building predictive
models, which

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.250
can be deployed into
our decisioning systems,

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.860
facilitating a full
ML ops ecosystem.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.940
The data lake that powers
Ignite is built on BigQuery.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.200
Our enterprise catalog services
are built on Cloud SQL.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.250
This is what you see in
the middle of this picture.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.150
It houses all of the metadata
for our data features

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:14.512
and models, plus the rules
that are applied on our data.

00:19:14.512 --> 00:19:16.970
This provides the auditability
and the automated governance

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.200
capability.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.450
This design gives Equifax
a leading position

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:22.490
as a data analytics
and technology company

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:23.735
in a highly regulated space.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.880
Finally, I wanted to leave
you with some metrics of where

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:34.010
we will be when we are finally
migrated onto this platform.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:37.160
You're looking at 75-plus
data fabric services deployed

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:41.480
in seven regions supporting
the 24 countries we operate in.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:46.640
We would have 10 billion-plus
of observations keyed,

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:51.170
using almost 90,000-plus
clicking and linking rules,

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:55.250
enabling us to manage 800
million-plus unique identities

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:57.267
globally.

00:19:57.267 --> 00:19:59.600
We are well into our journey
with significant production

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:00.793
traffic coming soon.

00:20:00.793 --> 00:20:02.210
We have an aggressive
plan to grow

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.180
the company via acquisitions
and organically.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:07.310
The partnership
we've had with Google

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:09.200
through this entire
journey has been great.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:10.700
Both our engineering
teams have been

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:12.590
able to collaborate
closely to achieve

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:15.350
these capabilities and
the seamless access

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:20.130
to the product engineering
teams at Google have been key.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.850
We have invested in a long-term
relationship with Google

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:27.600
and look forward to a
symbiotic partnership.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:29.300
Thank you for having
me, and hope you

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:32.480
guys found this
presentation useful.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.280
ANDI GUTMANS: Kumar,
thank you for sharing

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:36.740
your story of data-driven
cloud transformation.

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.780
It's truly inspiring.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:41.120
We look forward to our
ongoing partnership

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.800
with you and Equifax.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.160
At heart, Google
is a data company.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:50.530
When it comes to
managing data, we

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:53.880
have to solve some of the
most difficult problems.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.820
We re-imagined
databases and invented

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:59.070
Spanner, a database
that gives you

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:03.060
the best of relational and
nonrelational databases.

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:06.900
Our databases benefit from
disaggregated computing storage

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:08.970
and our privately-owned
global network

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:13.360
to improve performance,
scalability, security,

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:15.600
and availability.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:19.110
What all this means is that when
you adopt our Cloud databases,

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:22.260
you immediately benefit from
our longstanding culture

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:25.610
of engineering excellence
and innovation.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:27.680
There is a lot more
exciting content

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:30.960
waiting for you in
the upcoming sessions.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.450
I invite you to join these
sessions to learn more

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.670
about Google Cloud databases.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:38.250
And I would like to
thank you for joining us

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.230
at Google Cloud Next.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.870
We hope to partner
with you to accelerate

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:45.570
your data-driven
business transformation.

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:47.480
Thank you.

